# q03 — the `extend` is two levels down, in a file the entry never imports.
#
# Probes: `main.lume` imports `report` and nothing else. `report.lume`
# imports `stats.core`, which is where both `extend`s live. If an `extend`
# travels with the import, the entry's lists and sets arrive with `n` and
# `listed` on them without the entry ever naming `stats.core`. Also probes
# a module-qualified interface (`core.Spread[Int]`) used as a bound.
#
# The thing I am least sure of: whether an `extend` travels one hop or all
# the way. "An `extend` travels with the import, so a library ships its
# conformances" reads as transitive to me; if it is one hop only, lines
# four to six below are unknown-method errors in `main.lume`.
#
# Meant to RUN.
#
# EXPECTED OUTPUT:
# 4 readings: 4 9 4 2
# 2 readings: 7 1
# 4
# 4
# 7 1
# 1 2 3

import report

def main:
  day1 = [4, 9, 4, 2]
  day2 = {7, 1}

  puts report.headline(day1)
  puts report.headline(day2)
  puts report.widest(day1, day2)

  # the extend's own methods, on types the entry never extended, from a
  # module the entry never named
  puts day1.n
  puts day2.listed
  puts [1, 2, 3].listed
